From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CeraLive <dev@ceralive.tv>
Date: Sun, 10 Aug 2026 09:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 13/26] media: rockchip: rkvenc: add gated deterministic fault injection for the negative paths

MOTIVATION. 0014-0017 fix negative paths a working board never
executes (supplier bind failure, refused clock enable, a session
closed mid-task, a worker cancelled under a lock) -- exactly where a
use-after-free or lock inversion hides, and exactly what ordinary
encoding cannot reach. Without a way to force each on demand, the only
evidence for those five patches would be source reasoning alone.

BEHAVIOUR. Adds three Kconfig symbols and one new file: six one-shot,
mode-0600 debugfs controls under /sys/kernel/debug/rkvenc-test/
(fail_service_attach_once, fail_ccu_attach_once, fail_irq_request_once,
fail_clock_enable_once, fail_session_alloc_once,
delay_task_completion_ms), each consumed via atomic_cmpxchg() and
paired with a read-only <name>_consumed counter so a harness can tell
a fired fault from an ignored knob. CONFIG_VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_HDMIRX_CERALIVE_TEST
and CONFIG_DMABUF_HEAPS_CERALIVE_TEST are added here as symbols only,
owning instrumentation 0017 and 0018 implement later.

NON-GOALS. Not a production feature: with all three symbols off this
patch contributes zero bytes of code, zero debugfs nodes and zero
symbols to the built modules -- checked, not asserted: a
production-config build produces no rkvenc_test.o and no
"rkvenc-test" string in rkvenc.ko. The image pipeline never builds
this: all three symbols are on manifests/kernel/forbidden-symbols.list,
enabled only by the opt-in `edge-test` variant its release workflow
refuses to publish.

PROVENANCE. First-party CeraLive test scaffolding for the imported
0001 driver; no upstream counterpart.

EVIDENCE POINTER. Run on a Rock 5B+ under KASAN and lockdep with every
fault forced in turn; see tests/rkvenc-unbind.sh, tests/rkvenc-fault-qa.sh
and docs/BOARD-QUALIFICATION.md.

First-party: authored by CeraLive against v7.1.7, with no upstream
counterpart in rockchip-rk3588-mainline-patches. The source of record is
ceralive/0013-rkvenc-ceralive-test-instrumentation.patch; patches/ is generated from it by
scripts/build-series.py, and scripts/verify-payload-parity.py holds it to the
same added/removed-line parity the upstream lane gets.

NOT upstream-bound: this targets the CeraLive device tree only and is not a
submission to linux-media, linux-rockchip or the fork parent. No Signed-off-by
is added, because a DCO assertion belongs to whoever actually submits it.

---
diff --git a/drivers/dma-buf/heaps/Kconfig b/drivers/dma-buf/heaps/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/dma-buf/heaps/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/dma-buf/heaps/Kconfig
@@ -37,3 +37,22 @@ config DMABUF_HEAPS_CMA
 	  Choose this option to enable dma-buf CMA heap. This heap is backed
 	  by the Contiguous Memory Allocator (CMA). If your system has these
 	  regions, you should say Y here.
+
+config DMABUF_HEAPS_CERALIVE_TEST
+	bool "CeraLive KUnit test for system-heap registration (TEST BUILDS ONLY)"
+	depends on DMABUF_HEAPS_SYSTEM
+	depends on KUNIT=y
+	default n
+	help
+	  Builds ceralive_system_heap_test, a KUnit suite that drives the system
+	  heap's registration sequence through an injected add function so a
+	  FAILING second registration can be observed without touching the heaps
+	  the running kernel actually exports.
+
+	  It exists because dma_heap_add() has no counterpart: there is no
+	  removal or rollback API at this kernel version, so a second
+	  registration that fails leaves the first heap registered. That partial
+	  state is the honest outcome and the test asserts it, rather than
+	  asserting an atomicity the API cannot provide.
+
+	  If unsure, say N.
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/Kconfig b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/Kconfig
@@ -15,3 +15,23 @@ config VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_RKVENC
 
 	  To compile this driver as a module, choose M here: the module
 	  will be called rkvenc.
+
+config VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_RKVENC_CERALIVE_TEST
+	bool "CeraLive deterministic fault injection for RKVENC (TEST BUILDS ONLY)"
+	depends on VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_RKVENC
+	depends on DEBUG_FS
+	default n
+	help
+	  Adds one-shot, default-off fault controls under
+	  /sys/kernel/debug/rkvenc-test/ that force the encoder's negative paths
+	  — service and CCU attach failure, IRQ request failure, clock-enable
+	  failure, session-allocation failure and a delayed task completion —
+	  exactly once each, so the probe-unwind, teardown and resource-error
+	  handling can be proven correct under KASAN and lockdep instead of
+	  argued from source.
+
+	  This is QA instrumentation, NOT a debugging aid for a deployed device:
+	  it exists so a bench board can be made to fail on demand. Enabling it
+	  in a shipped kernel means shipping a driver that can be told to break.
+
+	  If unsure, say N.
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/Makefile b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/Makefile
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/Makefile
@@ -1,2 +1,3 @@
 obj-$(CONFIG_VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_RKVENC) += rkvenc.o
 rkvenc-objs := rkvenc_drv.o rkvenc_hw.o rkvenc_service.o rkvenc_task.o rkvenc_iommu.o
+rkvenc-$(CONFIG_VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_RKVENC_CERALIVE_TEST) += rkvenc_test.o
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 #include <linux/string.h>
 
 #include "rkvenc_hw.h"
+#include "rkvenc_test.h"
 
 /* ---- PM ops ---- */
 static int __maybe_unused rkvenc_runtime_suspend(struct device *dev)
@@ -65,6 +66,17 @@ static int rkvenc_attach_ccu(struct device *dev, struct rkvenc_dev *enc)
 	struct platform_device *pdev;
 	struct rkvenc_ccu *ccu;
 
+	/*
+	 * Injected before ANY state is touched, so the expected result of a
+	 * forced CCU-attach failure is a core that never joined the CCU at all
+	 * -- which is what makes the probe unwind observable rather than
+	 * entangled with a half-completed attach.
+	 */
+	if (rkvenc_test_fail_ccu_attach()) {
+		dev_warn(dev, "test: forcing CCU attach failure\n");
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
 	np = of_parse_phandle(dev->of_node, "rockchip,ccu", 0);
 	if (!np || !of_device_is_available(np))
 		return -ENODEV;
@@ -143,6 +155,14 @@ static int rkvenc_attach_service(struct rkvenc_dev *enc)
 	u32 taskqueue_node = 0;
 	u32 resetgroup_node = 0;
 
+	/* Before the queue publication below, so a forced failure leaves the
+	 * service's core count and idle slots exactly as they were.
+	 */
+	if (rkvenc_test_fail_service_attach()) {
+		dev_warn(enc->dev, "test: forcing service attach failure\n");
+		return -ENOMEM;
+	}
+
 	np = of_parse_phandle(enc->dev->of_node, "rockchip,srv", 0);
 	if (!np || !of_device_is_available(np))
 		return -ENODEV;
@@ -336,8 +356,13 @@ static int rkvenc_core_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	}
 
 	/* Register IRQ */
-	ret = devm_request_irq(dev, enc->irq, rkvenc_hw_irq,
-			       IRQF_SHARED, dev_name(dev), enc);
+	if (rkvenc_test_fail_irq_request()) {
+		dev_warn(dev, "test: forcing IRQ request failure\n");
+		ret = -EBUSY;
+	} else {
+		ret = devm_request_irq(dev, enc->irq, rkvenc_hw_irq,
+				       IRQF_SHARED, dev_name(dev), enc);
+	}
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(dev, "register interrupt failed: %d\n", ret);
 		goto err_hw;
@@ -473,7 +498,26 @@ static struct platform_driver rkvenc_driver = {
 	},
 };
 
-module_platform_driver(rkvenc_driver);
+/*
+ * Expanded from module_platform_driver() so the test controls' debugfs
+ * directory has MODULE lifetime rather than device lifetime. Creating it in the
+ * service probe would destroy it on every unbind, and the unbind test cases are
+ * precisely the ones that need to re-arm a control while the driver is
+ * detached. Both calls compile to nothing when the test symbol is off.
+ */
+static int __init rkvenc_module_init(void)
+{
+	rkvenc_test_init();
+	return platform_driver_register(&rkvenc_driver);
+}
+module_init(rkvenc_module_init);
+
+static void __exit rkvenc_module_exit(void)
+{
+	platform_driver_unregister(&rkvenc_driver);
+	rkvenc_test_exit();
+}
+module_exit(rkvenc_module_exit);
 
 MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Rockchip VEPU580 (RKVENC v2) H.265/H.264/JPEG encoder driver");
 MODULE_LICENSE("Dual MIT/GPL");
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.c
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 
 #include "rkvenc_hw.h"
+#include "rkvenc_test.h"
 
 unsigned int rkvenc_debug;
 module_param_named(debug, rkvenc_debug, uint, 0644);
@@ -510,6 +511,20 @@ int rkvenc_hw_run(struct rkvenc_dev *enc, struct rkvenc_mpp_task *mpp_task)
 		pm_runtime_get_sync(&enc->iommu_info->pdev->dev);
 	pm_runtime_get_sync(enc->dev);
 	pm_stay_awake(enc->dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * Injected AFTER the PM references are taken and BEFORE the clocks are
+	 * enabled, so err_pm below unwinds exactly the references this function
+	 * acquired -- the point of the case is that the PM and clock counts
+	 * stay balanced across a failed run, which a fault injected outside the
+	 * acquire/release pair could not test.
+	 */
+	if (rkvenc_test_fail_clock_enable()) {
+		rkvenc_err("test: forcing clock enable failure\n");
+		ret = -EIO;
+		goto err_pm;
+	}
+
 	rkvenc_hw_clk_on(enc);
 
 	/* Reset group down_read if available */
@@ -595,6 +610,7 @@ int rkvenc_hw_run(struct rkvenc_dev *enc, struct rkvenc_mpp_task *mpp_task)
 	if (enc->reset_group)
 		up_read(&enc->reset_group->rw_sem);
 	rkvenc_hw_clk_off(enc);
+err_pm:
 	pm_relax(enc->dev);
 	pm_runtime_mark_last_busy(enc->dev);
 	pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(enc->dev);
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@
 #include <linux/wait.h>
 
 #include "rkvenc_hw.h"
+#include "rkvenc_test.h"
 
 /* ---- MSG v1 structure (from userspace) ---- */
 struct mpp_msg_v1 {
@@ -75,6 +76,11 @@ static void rkvenc_session_deinit(struct rkvenc_session *session)
 static int rkvenc_session_attach_device(struct rkvenc_session *session,
 					struct rkvenc_dev *mpp)
 {
+	if (rkvenc_test_fail_session_alloc()) {
+		rkvenc_err("test: forcing session allocation failure\n");
+		return -ENOMEM;
+	}
+
 	session->mpp = mpp;
 	session->dma = rkvenc_dma_session_create(mpp->dev,
 						 mpp->session_max_buffers);
@@ -569,6 +575,7 @@ void rkvenc_task_worker_default(struct kthread_work *work)
 	struct rkvenc_mpp_task *mpp_task;
 	struct rkvenc_task *task;
 	struct rkvenc_session *session;
+	unsigned int delay_ms;
 
 	/* Process finished tasks (ISR bottom half) */
 	mpp_task = enc->cur_task;
@@ -594,6 +601,16 @@ void rkvenc_task_worker_default(struct kthread_work *work)
 		if (task->irq_status & enc->hw_info->err_mask)
 			atomic_inc(&enc->reset_request);
 
+		/*
+		 * Stretches the window in which a task is finished but its
+		 * session may already be closing. This is a kthread worker, so
+		 * sleeping here is legal; the delay is one-shot and reads back
+		 * 0, so it cannot leak into the next task.
+		 */
+		delay_ms = rkvenc_test_task_completion_delay_ms();
+		if (delay_ms)
+			msleep(delay_ms);
+
 		rkvenc_task_finish(session, mpp_task);
 
 		set_bit(enc->core_id, &queue->core_idle);
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_test.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_test.c
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_test.c
@@ -0,0 +1,137 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0+ OR MIT)
+/*
+ * Rockchip VEPU580 encoder driver - CeraLive deterministic fault injection
+ *
+ * Compiled ONLY under CONFIG_VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_RKVENC_CERALIVE_TEST. See
+ * rkvenc_test.h for the contract every control below implements.
+ *
+ * Copyright (C) 2026 CeraLive
+ */
+
+#include <linux/atomic.h>
+#include <linux/debugfs.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+
+#include "rkvenc_test.h"
+
+#define RKVENC_TEST_DIR_NAME "rkvenc-test"
+
+/*
+ * One-shot armed flags. atomic_cmpxchg() is what makes "one-shot" true rather
+ * than merely intended: two cores can probe concurrently and two sessions can
+ * open concurrently, so a read-then-clear would let a single armed fault fire
+ * twice and leave the consumed counter disagreeing with what actually happened.
+ */
+struct rkvenc_test_knob {
+	atomic_t armed;
+	atomic_t consumed;
+};
+
+static struct rkvenc_test_knob fail_service_attach;
+static struct rkvenc_test_knob fail_ccu_attach;
+static struct rkvenc_test_knob fail_irq_request;
+static struct rkvenc_test_knob fail_clock_enable;
+static struct rkvenc_test_knob fail_session_alloc;
+
+/*
+ * The delay is a VALUE knob rather than a flag, so its armed field carries
+ * milliseconds and is consumed with an unconditional exchange to 0.
+ */
+static struct rkvenc_test_knob delay_task_completion;
+
+static struct dentry *rkvenc_test_dir;
+
+static bool rkvenc_test_consume_flag(struct rkvenc_test_knob *knob)
+{
+	if (atomic_cmpxchg(&knob->armed, 1, 0) != 1)
+		return false;
+
+	atomic_inc(&knob->consumed);
+	return true;
+}
+
+static void rkvenc_test_add_knob(const char *name,
+				 struct rkvenc_test_knob *knob)
+{
+	char consumed_name[64];
+
+	debugfs_create_atomic_t(name, 0600, rkvenc_test_dir, &knob->armed);
+	snprintf(consumed_name, sizeof(consumed_name), "%s_consumed", name);
+	debugfs_create_atomic_t(consumed_name, 0400, rkvenc_test_dir,
+				&knob->consumed);
+}
+
+void rkvenc_test_init(void)
+{
+	if (rkvenc_test_dir)
+		return;
+
+	rkvenc_test_dir = debugfs_create_dir(RKVENC_TEST_DIR_NAME, NULL);
+
+	rkvenc_test_add_knob("fail_service_attach_once", &fail_service_attach);
+	rkvenc_test_add_knob("fail_ccu_attach_once", &fail_ccu_attach);
+	rkvenc_test_add_knob("fail_irq_request_once", &fail_irq_request);
+	rkvenc_test_add_knob("fail_clock_enable_once", &fail_clock_enable);
+	rkvenc_test_add_knob("fail_session_alloc_once", &fail_session_alloc);
+
+	/*
+	 * The delay knob's counter is named `delay_consumed` rather than
+	 * `delay_task_completion_ms_consumed`: the QA harness and the plan's
+	 * fault-control table both name it that way, and an "_ms_consumed"
+	 * counter would read as a duration, which it is not.
+	 */
+	debugfs_create_atomic_t("delay_task_completion_ms", 0600,
+				rkvenc_test_dir, &delay_task_completion.armed);
+	debugfs_create_atomic_t("delay_consumed", 0400, rkvenc_test_dir,
+				&delay_task_completion.consumed);
+
+	pr_info("rkvenc: CeraLive test controls at /sys/kernel/debug/%s (NOT a production kernel)\n",
+		RKVENC_TEST_DIR_NAME);
+}
+
+void rkvenc_test_exit(void)
+{
+	debugfs_remove_recursive(rkvenc_test_dir);
+	rkvenc_test_dir = NULL;
+}
+
+bool rkvenc_test_fail_service_attach(void)
+{
+	return rkvenc_test_consume_flag(&fail_service_attach);
+}
+
+bool rkvenc_test_fail_ccu_attach(void)
+{
+	return rkvenc_test_consume_flag(&fail_ccu_attach);
+}
+
+bool rkvenc_test_fail_irq_request(void)
+{
+	return rkvenc_test_consume_flag(&fail_irq_request);
+}
+
+bool rkvenc_test_fail_clock_enable(void)
+{
+	return rkvenc_test_consume_flag(&fail_clock_enable);
+}
+
+bool rkvenc_test_fail_session_alloc(void)
+{
+	return rkvenc_test_consume_flag(&fail_session_alloc);
+}
+
+unsigned int rkvenc_test_task_completion_delay_ms(void)
+{
+	int ms = atomic_xchg(&delay_task_completion.armed, 0);
+
+	/*
+	 * A negative value can only come from a debugfs write, and treating it
+	 * as a huge unsigned delay would wedge the encoder worker. Consume it
+	 * (so the knob still auto-resets) and report no delay.
+	 */
+	if (ms <= 0)
+		return 0;
+
+	atomic_inc(&delay_task_completion.consumed);
+	return (unsigned int)ms;
+}
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_test.h b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_test.h
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_test.h
@@ -0,0 +1,60 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0+ OR MIT) */
+/*
+ * Rockchip VEPU580 encoder driver - CeraLive deterministic fault injection
+ *
+ * PRODUCTION-ABSENT BY CONSTRUCTION. Every entry point below is a static inline
+ * that returns a compile-time constant when CONFIG_VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_RKVENC_CERALIVE_TEST
+ * is off, so each call site collapses to nothing at all: no branch, no symbol,
+ * no debugfs directory, no dead code. The whole implementation lives in
+ * rkvenc_test.c, which the Makefile only compiles under that symbol.
+ *
+ * WHY THIS EXISTS. The teardown, resource-error and bounds work in this series
+ * fixes paths that a working board never takes: a supplier that fails to bind, a
+ * clock that refuses to enable, a task still in flight when its session is
+ * closed. Those paths cannot be proven correct by inspection and cannot be
+ * reached by any amount of ordinary encoding, so each one gets a one-shot,
+ * default-off control that forces it exactly once under KASAN and lockdep.
+ *
+ * CONTRACT (identical for every control, and the QA harnesses depend on it):
+ *   - the knob lives at /sys/kernel/debug/rkvenc-test/<name>, mode 0600;
+ *   - it is ONE-SHOT: the driver consumes it atomically and it reads back 0,
+ *     so a fault can never leak into the next operation or the next test case;
+ *   - each knob has a read-only <name>_consumed counter that increments exactly
+ *     once per consumption, which is what lets a harness distinguish "the fault
+ *     fired and the driver handled it" from "the fault never fired".
+ *
+ * Copyright (C) 2026 CeraLive
+ */
+
+#ifndef __RKVENC_TEST_H__
+#define __RKVENC_TEST_H__
+
+#include <linux/types.h>
+
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_RKVENC_CERALIVE_TEST)
+
+void rkvenc_test_init(void);
+void rkvenc_test_exit(void);
+
+bool rkvenc_test_fail_service_attach(void);
+bool rkvenc_test_fail_ccu_attach(void);
+bool rkvenc_test_fail_irq_request(void);
+bool rkvenc_test_fail_clock_enable(void);
+bool rkvenc_test_fail_session_alloc(void);
+unsigned int rkvenc_test_task_completion_delay_ms(void);
+
+#else /* !CONFIG_VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_RKVENC_CERALIVE_TEST */
+
+static inline void rkvenc_test_init(void) { }
+static inline void rkvenc_test_exit(void) { }
+
+static inline bool rkvenc_test_fail_service_attach(void) { return false; }
+static inline bool rkvenc_test_fail_ccu_attach(void) { return false; }
+static inline bool rkvenc_test_fail_irq_request(void) { return false; }
+static inline bool rkvenc_test_fail_clock_enable(void) { return false; }
+static inline bool rkvenc_test_fail_session_alloc(void) { return false; }
+static inline unsigned int rkvenc_test_task_completion_delay_ms(void) { return 0; }
+
+#endif /* CONFIG_VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_RKVENC_CERALIVE_TEST */
+
+#endif /* __RKVENC_TEST_H__ */
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/synopsys/hdmirx/Kconfig b/drivers/media/platform/synopsys/hdmirx/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/media/platform/synopsys/hdmirx/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/synopsys/hdmirx/Kconfig
@@ -35,3 +35,21 @@ config VIDEO_SYNOPSYS_HDMIRX_LOAD_DEFAULT_EDID
 	  non-functional until EDID will be loaded from userspace.
 	  Which is a wanted behavior when using this driver in a
 	  commercial product that should utilize own branded EDID.
+
+config VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_HDMIRX_CERALIVE_TEST
+	bool "CeraLive deterministic fault injection for HDMI-RX audio (TEST BUILDS ONLY)"
+	depends on VIDEO_SYNOPSYS_HDMIRX
+	depends on DEBUG_FS
+	default n
+	help
+	  Adds one-shot, default-off controls under
+	  /sys/kernel/debug/hdmirx-audio-test/ that stretch the audio worker and
+	  force a single audio clock-rate failure, so the worker/clock lifecycle
+	  can be exercised under lockdep — a synchronous cancel racing a running
+	  worker, and an error return the driver used to discard.
+
+	  This is QA instrumentation for a bench board, not a field debugging
+	  aid. Enabling it in a shipped kernel means shipping a receiver that
+	  can be told to fail.
+
+	  If unsure, say N.
